1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,031
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,211 --> 00:00:04,582
Most people who stay broke aren't lazy,

3
00:00:04,582 --> 00:00:06,588
and they aren't bad with money in

4
00:00:06,588 --> 00:00:07,621
some obvious way.

5
00:00:07,801 --> 00:00:08,881
They work hard.

6
00:00:09,061 --> 00:00:10,454
They pay their bills.

7
00:00:10,634 --> 00:00:12,367
And still, every month,

8
00:00:12,367 --> 00:00:14,326
the money just disappears.

9
00:00:14,506 --> 00:00:16,777
The reason is usually not one big

10
00:00:16,777 --> 00:00:17,328
mistake.

11
00:00:17,508 --> 00:00:19,748
It's a handful of small habits that

12
00:00:19,748 --> 00:00:21,669
run quietly in the background,

13
00:00:21,669 --> 00:00:24,102
like apps draining your phone battery.

14
00:00:24,282 --> 00:00:26,163
Today, twenty five of them.

15
00:00:26,513 --> 00:00:27,882
Here's why this matters.

16
00:00:28,062 --> 00:00:30,982
In the Federal Reserve's survey of household

17
00:00:30,982 --> 00:00:33,237
well being for twenty twenty four,

18
00:00:33,237 --> 00:00:35,891
about sixty three percent of adults said

19
00:00:35,891 --> 00:00:38,412
they would cover a four hundred dollar

20
00:00:38,412 --> 00:00:41,530
emergency expense using cash or its equivalent.

21
00:00:41,710 --> 00:00:43,538
That means more than a third would

22
00:00:43,538 --> 00:00:43,753
not.

23
00:00:44,104 --> 00:00:45,067
Think about that.

24
00:00:45,247 --> 00:00:47,557
Tens of millions of working adults,

25
00:00:47,557 --> 00:00:49,010
many with decent jobs,

26
00:00:49,010 --> 00:00:50,396
would have to borrow,

27
00:00:50,396 --> 00:00:52,772
sell something, or simply not pay if

28
00:00:52,772 --> 00:00:55,149
a four hundred dollar bill showed up

29
00:00:55,149 --> 00:00:55,743
tomorrow.

30
00:00:55,923 --> 00:00:58,337
That's not usually a salary problem.

31
00:00:58,517 --> 00:01:00,664
Plenty of people with good incomes are

32
00:01:00,664 --> 00:01:01,455
in that group.

33
00:01:01,635 --> 00:01:02,944
It's a habits problem,

34
00:01:02,944 --> 00:01:04,491
and habits can be changed.

35
00:01:04,841 --> 00:01:07,086
We'll look at these twenty five habits

36
00:01:07,086 --> 00:01:08,149
from three angles.

37
00:01:08,329 --> 00:01:09,746
First, the numbers.

38
00:01:09,926 --> 00:01:11,816
Habits with a cost you can actually

39
00:01:11,816 --> 00:01:12,248
measure.

40
00:01:12,428 --> 00:01:14,355
Second, the psychology.

41
00:01:14,535 --> 00:01:16,990
The mental shortcuts that keep you spending

42
00:01:16,990 --> 00:01:17,960
without noticing.

43
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:19,673
And third, the playbook.

44
00:01:19,853 --> 00:01:22,301
The missing systems that keep people stuck,

45
00:01:22,301 --> 00:01:24,009
and what to replace them with.

46
00:01:24,189 --> 00:01:25,202
Stay to the end,

47
00:01:25,202 --> 00:01:27,608
because the last angle is where things

48
00:01:27,608 --> 00:01:28,937
start to turn around.

49
00:01:29,288 --> 00:01:30,878
Let's start with the numbers.

50
00:01:31,058 --> 00:01:33,844
And habit number one is something almost

51
00:01:33,844 --> 00:01:36,074
everyone does, every single day.

52
00:01:36,424 --> 00:01:38,792
Our numbers come from named sources.

53
00:01:38,972 --> 00:01:41,968
Household emergency data comes from the Federal

54
00:01:41,968 --> 00:01:45,729
Reserve's Survey of Household Economics and Decisionmaking.

55
00:01:45,909 --> 00:01:48,365
Credit card rates come from the Federal

56
00:01:48,365 --> 00:01:50,507
Reserve's consumer credit reports.

57
00:01:50,687 --> 00:01:53,111
Banking fee data comes from the Consumer

58
00:01:53,111 --> 00:01:54,808
Financial Protection Bureau.

59
00:01:54,988 --> 00:01:57,699
Growth examples use simple stated math,

60
00:01:57,699 --> 00:02:00,689
like a seven percent average yearly return.

61
00:02:00,869 --> 00:02:02,427
That's an illustration,

62
00:02:02,427 --> 00:02:03,376
not a promise.

63
00:02:03,556 --> 00:02:04,885
This is education,

64
00:02:04,885 --> 00:02:06,436
not financial advice.

65
00:02:06,786 --> 00:02:07,598
Angle one.

66
00:02:07,778 --> 00:02:08,463
The numbers.

67
00:02:08,643 --> 00:02:10,590
Nine habits where the cost is right

68
00:02:10,590 --> 00:02:11,981
there in black and white,

69
00:02:11,981 --> 00:02:12,649
if you look.

70
00:02:12,999 --> 00:02:14,009
Habit number one.

71
00:02:14,189 --> 00:02:16,696
Not knowing where your money actually goes.

72
00:02:17,047 --> 00:02:18,907
Ask most people what they spend in

73
00:02:18,907 --> 00:02:19,782
a month on food,

74
00:02:19,782 --> 00:02:21,423
and they'll give you a number.

75
00:02:21,603 --> 00:02:23,576
Ask them to check their statements,

76
00:02:23,576 --> 00:02:25,435
and the real number is often much

77
00:02:25,435 --> 00:02:25,830
higher.

78
00:02:26,010 --> 00:02:27,438
It's not dishonesty.

79
00:02:27,618 --> 00:02:29,905
Small purchases are easy to forget.

80
00:02:30,085 --> 00:02:31,199
A coffee here,

81
00:02:31,199 --> 00:02:32,314
a snack there,

82
00:02:32,314 --> 00:02:33,986
a quick online order.

83
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:35,838
None of them feel like spending.

84
00:02:36,188 --> 00:02:38,080
You can't fix what you can't see.

85
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:40,902
People who build wealth almost always know

86
00:02:40,902 --> 00:02:42,916
their numbers, at least roughly.

87
00:02:43,096 --> 00:02:45,520
Try this: for the next thirty days,

88
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:47,459
track every single purchase,

89
00:02:47,459 --> 00:02:49,191
even the one dollar ones.

90
00:02:49,371 --> 00:02:51,114
Use an app or a note on

91
00:02:51,114 --> 00:02:51,948
your phone.

92
00:02:52,128 --> 00:02:52,978
At the end,

93
00:02:52,978 --> 00:02:55,913
sort them into five or six categories.

94
00:02:56,093 --> 00:02:57,998
The biggest surprise on that list is

95
00:02:57,998 --> 00:02:58,845
where you start.

96
00:02:59,195 --> 00:03:00,286
Habit number two.

97
00:03:00,466 --> 00:03:02,440
Carrying a balance on your credit card.

98
00:03:02,790 --> 00:03:04,812
According to the Federal Reserve,

99
00:03:04,812 --> 00:03:07,264
the average rate on credit card accounts

100
00:03:07,264 --> 00:03:09,654
that pay interest has been above twenty

101
00:03:09,654 --> 00:03:11,125
percent in recent years.

102
00:03:11,305 --> 00:03:13,258
On a five thousand dollar balance,

103
00:03:13,258 --> 00:03:15,554
that's roughly a thousand dollars a year

104
00:03:15,554 --> 00:03:16,588
in interest alone.

105
00:03:16,768 --> 00:03:18,681
And if you only make the minimum

106
00:03:18,681 --> 00:03:20,713
payment, it can take many years to

107
00:03:20,713 --> 00:03:21,191
pay off.

108
00:03:21,542 --> 00:03:24,018
This habit keeps people broke because it

109
00:03:24,018 --> 00:03:25,814
works in reverse compounding.

110
00:03:25,994 --> 00:03:28,334
Instead of your money growing for you,

111
00:03:28,334 --> 00:03:30,058
your debt grows against you.

112
00:03:30,238 --> 00:03:32,606
Try this: list every card with its

113
00:03:32,606 --> 00:03:33,790
balance and rate.

114
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:35,802
Pay the minimum on all of them,

115
00:03:35,802 --> 00:03:37,751
and put every extra dollar on the

116
00:03:37,751 --> 00:03:39,288
one with the highest rate.

117
00:03:39,468 --> 00:03:40,310
When it's gone,

118
00:03:40,310 --> 00:03:42,219
roll that payment to the next one.

119
00:03:42,399 --> 00:03:44,419
It's called the avalanche method.

120
00:03:44,770 --> 00:03:45,954
Habit number three.

121
00:03:46,134 --> 00:03:48,068
Paying bank fees as if they were

122
00:03:48,068 --> 00:03:48,491
normal.

123
00:03:48,841 --> 00:03:52,179
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has reported

124
00:03:52,179 --> 00:03:54,825
that banks collected billions of dollars a

125
00:03:54,825 --> 00:03:57,470
year in overdraft and non sufficient funds

126
00:03:57,470 --> 00:03:59,423
fees, and that a small share of

127
00:03:59,423 --> 00:04:01,124
customers pay most of them.

128
00:04:01,304 --> 00:04:03,963
An overdraft fee has often been around

129
00:04:03,963 --> 00:04:05,573
thirty dollars or more,

130
00:04:05,573 --> 00:04:07,883
sometimes on a purchase of just a

131
00:04:07,883 --> 00:04:08,723
few dollars.

132
00:04:09,073 --> 00:04:11,835
Many banks and credit unions now offer

133
00:04:11,835 --> 00:04:14,379
accounts with no monthly fee and no

134
00:04:14,379 --> 00:04:15,470
overdraft fees.

135
00:04:15,650 --> 00:04:17,507
Switching takes an afternoon.

136
00:04:17,687 --> 00:04:19,667
Try this: look at your last six

137
00:04:19,667 --> 00:04:22,029
months of statements and add up every

138
00:04:22,029 --> 00:04:22,285
fee.

139
00:04:22,465 --> 00:04:24,328
Then call your bank and ask them

140
00:04:24,328 --> 00:04:26,017
to waive the most recent one.

141
00:04:26,197 --> 00:04:27,730
Many will, once.

142
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:29,712
And if your bank won't stop charging

143
00:04:29,712 --> 00:04:31,764
you, consider moving to one that doesn't.

144
00:04:32,115 --> 00:04:33,125
Habit number four.

145
00:04:33,305 --> 00:04:35,035
Thinking in monthly payments.

146
00:04:35,385 --> 00:04:38,698
Car dealers, phone carriers and even furniture

147
00:04:38,698 --> 00:04:41,004
stores love to ask one question.

148
00:04:41,184 --> 00:04:43,239
What monthly payment are you comfortable with?

149
00:04:43,419 --> 00:04:45,908
Once you think in payments instead of

150
00:04:45,908 --> 00:04:49,003
total price, almost anything feels affordable.

151
00:04:49,183 --> 00:04:51,997
Edmunds has reported average new car payments

152
00:04:51,997 --> 00:04:54,498
well above seven hundred dollars a month

153
00:04:54,498 --> 00:04:55,499
in recent years.

154
00:04:55,849 --> 00:04:57,857
The problem is that payments stack.

155
00:04:58,037 --> 00:04:59,436
A car, a phone,

156
00:04:59,436 --> 00:05:01,115
a couch, a laptop,

157
00:05:01,115 --> 00:05:03,540
a buy now pay later order.

158
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:05,531
Each one feels small.

159
00:05:05,711 --> 00:05:07,584
Together they can eat a huge share

160
00:05:07,584 --> 00:05:09,622
of your income before you've bought a

161
00:05:09,622 --> 00:05:10,448
single grocery.

162
00:05:10,628 --> 00:05:13,228
Try this: add up every monthly payment

163
00:05:13,228 --> 00:05:15,760
you have, excluding rent or mortgage.

164
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:17,448
Write the total on a sticky note

165
00:05:17,448 --> 00:05:19,098
and put it somewhere you'll see it.

166
00:05:19,448 --> 00:05:20,690
Habit number five.

167
00:05:20,870 --> 00:05:23,122
Ignoring small recurring charges.

168
00:05:23,473 --> 00:05:26,630
Streaming, music, cloud storage,

169
00:05:26,630 --> 00:05:29,295
apps, delivery memberships,

170
00:05:29,295 --> 00:05:30,775
gym, meal kits.

171
00:05:30,955 --> 00:05:32,627
Each one is a few dollars a

172
00:05:32,627 --> 00:05:32,999
month.

173
00:05:33,179 --> 00:05:36,522
Surveys regularly find that people underestimate what

174
00:05:36,522 --> 00:05:38,289
they spend on subscriptions,

175
00:05:38,289 --> 00:05:39,866
sometimes by quite a lot.

176
00:05:40,046 --> 00:05:42,285
Companies count on auto renewal and on

177
00:05:42,285 --> 00:05:43,169
you forgetting.

178
00:05:43,519 --> 00:05:45,950
Ten dollars a month doesn't sound like

179
00:05:45,950 --> 00:05:46,270
much.

180
00:05:46,450 --> 00:05:48,795
But ten subscriptions at ten dollars is

181
00:05:48,795 --> 00:05:50,479
one hundred dollars a month,

182
00:05:50,479 --> 00:05:51,802
twelve hundred a year.

183
00:05:51,982 --> 00:05:54,562
Try this: search your statements for every

184
00:05:54,562 --> 00:05:55,790
charge that repeats.

185
00:05:55,970 --> 00:05:56,901
Next to each one,

186
00:05:56,901 --> 00:05:58,652
write the last date you used it.

187
00:05:58,832 --> 00:06:00,701
Cancel anything older than a month.

188
00:06:00,881 --> 00:06:02,077
If you really miss it,

189
00:06:02,077 --> 00:06:03,598
you can sign up again later.

190
00:06:03,949 --> 00:06:05,202
Habit number six.

191
00:06:05,382 --> 00:06:07,356
Eating out because there's no plan.

192
00:06:07,706 --> 00:06:09,668
Food away from home is one of

193
00:06:09,668 --> 00:06:13,323
the biggest budget categories for American households,

194
00:06:13,323 --> 00:06:16,232
according to the Bureau of Labor Statistics

195
00:06:16,232 --> 00:06:17,992
consumer expenditure data.

196
00:06:18,172 --> 00:06:20,006
Much of it isn't special dinners.

197
00:06:20,186 --> 00:06:22,274
It's the default choice at the end

198
00:06:22,274 --> 00:06:23,134
of a long day,

199
00:06:23,134 --> 00:06:25,283
when there's nothing ready at home.

200
00:06:25,634 --> 00:06:27,456
The fix isn't to never eat out.

201
00:06:27,636 --> 00:06:29,210
It's to make eating at home the

202
00:06:29,210 --> 00:06:29,819
easy option.

203
00:06:29,999 --> 00:06:31,258
Try this: on Sunday,

204
00:06:31,258 --> 00:06:33,588
plan four simple dinners for the week

205
00:06:33,588 --> 00:06:35,478
and buy only what you need for

206
00:06:35,478 --> 00:06:35,792
them.

207
00:06:35,972 --> 00:06:38,712
Cook double portions so you have lunches.

208
00:06:38,892 --> 00:06:41,383
If you replace even three takeout meals

209
00:06:41,383 --> 00:06:43,490
a week, the yearly savings can be

210
00:06:43,490 --> 00:06:45,853
well over a thousand dollars for many

211
00:06:45,853 --> 00:06:46,427
families.

212
00:06:46,778 --> 00:06:47,892
Habit number seven.

213
00:06:48,072 --> 00:06:50,697
Leaving savings in an account that pays

214
00:06:50,697 --> 00:06:51,706
almost nothing.

215
00:06:52,056 --> 00:06:54,925
Many traditional savings accounts pay a tiny

216
00:06:54,925 --> 00:06:57,925
interest rate, while online high yield savings

217
00:06:57,925 --> 00:07:01,055
accounts have offered rates several times higher

218
00:07:01,055 --> 00:07:02,098
in recent years.

219
00:07:02,278 --> 00:07:04,527
When inflation runs at a few percent

220
00:07:04,527 --> 00:07:06,776
and your savings earn close to zero,

221
00:07:06,776 --> 00:07:09,212
your money is losing buying power every

222
00:07:09,212 --> 00:07:11,649
year, even though the balance looks the

223
00:07:11,649 --> 00:07:11,961
same.

224
00:07:12,311 --> 00:07:14,813
Your emergency fund should be safe and

225
00:07:14,813 --> 00:07:15,735
easy to reach,

226
00:07:15,735 --> 00:07:18,303
so a high yield savings account insured

227
00:07:18,303 --> 00:07:20,345
by the FDIC is a common choice.

228
00:07:20,525 --> 00:07:22,458
Try this: check the rate on your

229
00:07:22,458 --> 00:07:23,787
savings account today.

230
00:07:23,967 --> 00:07:25,840
Then compare it with a few FDIC

231
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:27,531
insured high yield accounts.

232
00:07:27,711 --> 00:07:28,983
If the gap is big,

233
00:07:28,983 --> 00:07:32,093
moving the money takes about fifteen minutes

234
00:07:32,093 --> 00:07:33,365
and costs nothing.

235
00:07:33,716 --> 00:07:34,865
Habit number eight.

236
00:07:35,045 --> 00:07:36,624
Paying bills late.

237
00:07:36,974 --> 00:07:39,110
Late payments cost twice.

238
00:07:39,290 --> 00:07:41,040
First, the late fee itself,

239
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:43,308
which can be thirty dollars or more

240
00:07:43,308 --> 00:07:44,410
on a credit card.

241
00:07:44,590 --> 00:07:47,057
Second, if a payment is reported as

242
00:07:47,057 --> 00:07:48,819
thirty days late or more,

243
00:07:48,819 --> 00:07:51,145
it can hurt your credit score for

244
00:07:51,145 --> 00:07:51,567
years.

245
00:07:51,747 --> 00:07:54,439
A lower score means higher rates on

246
00:07:54,439 --> 00:07:58,284
car loans, mortgages and sometimes even insurance.

247
00:07:58,634 --> 00:08:01,208
Most late payments aren't because people can't

248
00:08:01,208 --> 00:08:01,432
pay.

249
00:08:01,612 --> 00:08:02,587
They just forget.

250
00:08:02,767 --> 00:08:04,843
Try this: set every bill you can

251
00:08:04,843 --> 00:08:07,179
to automatic payment of at least the

252
00:08:07,179 --> 00:08:09,710
minimum, timed a few days after payday.

253
00:08:09,890 --> 00:08:12,170
Then set a monthly reminder to review

254
00:08:12,170 --> 00:08:12,479
them.

255
00:08:12,659 --> 00:08:14,424
Automation removes the forgetting,

256
00:08:14,424 --> 00:08:16,293
and the review keeps you in control.

257
00:08:16,643 --> 00:08:17,757
Habit number nine.

258
00:08:17,937 --> 00:08:19,261
Always buying new.

259
00:08:19,611 --> 00:08:22,031
Cars, furniture, tools,

260
00:08:22,031 --> 00:08:24,978
baby gear, sports equipment,

261
00:08:24,978 --> 00:08:26,031
textbooks.

262
00:08:26,211 --> 00:08:28,072
Many of these lose a big part

263
00:08:28,072 --> 00:08:30,382
of their value right after the first

264
00:08:30,382 --> 00:08:30,959
purchase.

265
00:08:31,139 --> 00:08:33,316
Buying lightly used lets someone else pay

266
00:08:33,316 --> 00:08:34,379
for that first drop.

267
00:08:34,559 --> 00:08:36,918
For cars, the first few years usually

268
00:08:36,918 --> 00:08:38,959
bring the biggest loss in value.

269
00:08:39,309 --> 00:08:40,783
This doesn't mean buying junk.

270
00:08:40,963 --> 00:08:43,349
It means checking the used market first,

271
00:08:43,349 --> 00:08:45,259
especially for things that last.

272
00:08:45,439 --> 00:08:48,054
Try this: next time you need something

273
00:08:48,054 --> 00:08:49,775
over one hundred dollars,

274
00:08:49,775 --> 00:08:53,009
spend ten minutes checking used options locally

275
00:08:53,009 --> 00:08:55,005
or online before you buy new.

276
00:08:55,185 --> 00:08:56,157
Over a lifetime,

277
00:08:56,157 --> 00:08:57,858
that one habit can add up to

278
00:08:57,858 --> 00:08:59,620
tens of thousands of dollars.

279
00:08:59,971 --> 00:09:01,235
Before we move on,

280
00:09:01,235 --> 00:09:03,344
let's do the math on why these

281
00:09:03,344 --> 00:09:05,311
quiet habits matter so much.

282
00:09:05,491 --> 00:09:08,737
Benjamin Franklin supposedly warned to beware of

283
00:09:08,737 --> 00:09:11,577
little expenses, because a small leak will

284
00:09:11,577 --> 00:09:12,794
sink a great ship.

285
00:09:12,974 --> 00:09:15,495
Whether he said it exactly that way

286
00:09:15,495 --> 00:09:17,223
or not, the math agrees.

287
00:09:17,403 --> 00:09:19,595
A six dollar daily habit is over

288
00:09:19,595 --> 00:09:21,513
two thousand dollars a year.

289
00:09:21,693 --> 00:09:24,132
A forty dollar monthly fee is almost

290
00:09:24,132 --> 00:09:25,013
five hundred.

291
00:09:25,193 --> 00:09:27,688
A credit card balance at twenty percent

292
00:09:27,688 --> 00:09:30,183
quietly takes a thousand dollars a year

293
00:09:30,183 --> 00:09:31,591
on five thousand owed.

294
00:09:31,941 --> 00:09:33,972
Now stack a few of those together.

295
00:09:34,152 --> 00:09:36,219
A forgotten subscription or two,

296
00:09:36,219 --> 00:09:37,898
a bank fee here and there,

297
00:09:37,898 --> 00:09:40,675
some interest, takeout three extra nights a

298
00:09:40,675 --> 00:09:42,936
week, and a car payment bigger than

299
00:09:42,936 --> 00:09:43,905
it needs to be.

300
00:09:44,085 --> 00:09:46,196
For many households that easily adds up

301
00:09:46,196 --> 00:09:48,090
to several hundred dollars a month.

302
00:09:48,270 --> 00:09:50,392
That's money that isn't going into an

303
00:09:50,392 --> 00:09:52,801
emergency fund, isn't paying down debt and

304
00:09:52,801 --> 00:09:54,005
isn't being invested.

305
00:09:54,185 --> 00:09:55,433
Month after month,

306
00:09:55,433 --> 00:09:56,542
year after year.

307
00:09:56,893 --> 00:09:58,936
And here's the part most people miss.

308
00:09:59,116 --> 00:10:01,325
Those leaks don't only cost what you

309
00:10:01,325 --> 00:10:01,693
spend.

310
00:10:01,873 --> 00:10:03,710
They cost what that money could have

311
00:10:03,710 --> 00:10:04,068
become.

312
00:10:04,248 --> 00:10:06,245
Every dollar that leaks out today is

313
00:10:06,245 --> 00:10:08,188
a dollar that never gets the chance

314
00:10:08,188 --> 00:10:10,074
to compound for the next twenty or

315
00:10:10,074 --> 00:10:10,796
thirty years.

316
00:10:10,976 --> 00:10:13,236
That's why fixing these habits in your

317
00:10:13,236 --> 00:10:15,377
twenties or thirties is so powerful,

318
00:10:15,377 --> 00:10:17,161
and why fixing them at any age

319
00:10:17,161 --> 00:10:18,232
is still worth it.

320
00:10:18,582 --> 00:10:21,020
Here's what fixing these first nine habits

321
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:21,832
can add up to.

322
00:10:22,012 --> 00:10:24,115
Say they free up four hundred dollars

323
00:10:24,115 --> 00:10:25,763
a month, and you invest it at

324
00:10:25,763 --> 00:10:27,922
a seven percent average yearly return.

325
00:10:28,102 --> 00:10:29,454
After twenty five years,

326
00:10:29,454 --> 00:10:32,214
that's roughly three hundred twenty four thousand

327
00:10:32,214 --> 00:10:32,665
dollars.

328
00:10:33,015 --> 00:10:34,071
That's angle one.

329
00:10:34,251 --> 00:10:36,143
Notice that none of these are dramatic.

330
00:10:36,323 --> 00:10:37,043
They're quiet.

331
00:10:37,223 --> 00:10:39,267
That's exactly why they're dangerous.

332
00:10:39,447 --> 00:10:40,612
Now angle two,

333
00:10:40,612 --> 00:10:41,862
the psychology.

334
00:10:42,042 --> 00:10:43,903
These are the mental habits that make

335
00:10:43,903 --> 00:10:45,211
the money habits possible.

336
00:10:45,561 --> 00:10:47,843
Spending decisions happen fast,

337
00:10:47,843 --> 00:10:49,095
often in seconds,

338
00:10:49,095 --> 00:10:51,598
and usually without much thinking.

339
00:10:51,778 --> 00:10:55,407
Behavioral economists, like Nobel Prize winners Daniel

340
00:10:55,407 --> 00:10:57,289
Kahneman and Richard Thaler,

341
00:10:57,289 --> 00:11:00,650
spent decades showing that humans aren't perfectly

342
00:11:00,650 --> 00:11:01,995
rational with money.

343
00:11:02,175 --> 00:11:03,730
We use shortcuts.

344
00:11:03,910 --> 00:11:06,429
Those shortcuts are useful in daily life,

345
00:11:06,429 --> 00:11:08,395
but some of them quietly keep us

346
00:11:08,395 --> 00:11:08,763
broke.

347
00:11:09,114 --> 00:11:10,333
Habit number ten.

348
00:11:10,513 --> 00:11:12,521
Treating extra money as free money.

349
00:11:12,871 --> 00:11:13,948
A tax refund,

350
00:11:13,948 --> 00:11:16,103
a bonus, a birthday check,

351
00:11:16,103 --> 00:11:17,677
a side gig payment.

352
00:11:17,857 --> 00:11:19,993
Thaler called this mental accounting.

353
00:11:20,173 --> 00:11:22,841
We put money into different mental buckets,

354
00:11:22,841 --> 00:11:25,384
and money that feels unexpected goes into

355
00:11:25,384 --> 00:11:26,315
the fun bucket.

356
00:11:26,495 --> 00:11:27,942
But a dollar is a dollar,

357
00:11:27,942 --> 00:11:30,084
whether it came from your paycheck or

358
00:11:30,084 --> 00:11:30,605
a refund.

359
00:11:30,955 --> 00:11:33,545
The IRS has reported average federal tax

360
00:11:33,545 --> 00:11:36,394
refunds of roughly three thousand dollars in

361
00:11:36,394 --> 00:11:37,236
recent years.

362
00:11:37,416 --> 00:11:38,560
For many households,

363
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:40,850
that's the single biggest chunk of money

364
00:11:40,850 --> 00:11:41,880
they get all year,

365
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:43,998
and it often disappears within weeks.

366
00:11:44,178 --> 00:11:47,156
Try this: before your next windfall arrives,

367
00:11:47,156 --> 00:11:49,322
decide in writing where it goes.

368
00:11:49,502 --> 00:11:52,408
For example, half to savings or debt,

369
00:11:52,408 --> 00:11:53,507
half to enjoy.

370
00:11:53,687 --> 00:11:55,475
Decide before you see the money.

371
00:11:55,825 --> 00:11:57,183
Habit number eleven.

372
00:11:57,363 --> 00:11:59,383
Rewarding yourself for everything.

373
00:11:59,733 --> 00:12:01,428
Hard day, buy something.

374
00:12:01,608 --> 00:12:03,279
Good day, buy something.

375
00:12:03,459 --> 00:12:04,723
Finished a project,

376
00:12:04,723 --> 00:12:05,654
buy something.

377
00:12:05,834 --> 00:12:06,967
Treats are fine,

378
00:12:06,967 --> 00:12:09,728
but when every feeling becomes a reason

379
00:12:09,728 --> 00:12:12,561
to spend, rewards stop being special and

380
00:12:12,561 --> 00:12:14,402
start being a budget line.

381
00:12:14,582 --> 00:12:16,979
Many people don't notice how often they

382
00:12:16,979 --> 00:12:18,761
use the words I deserve this.

383
00:12:19,112 --> 00:12:21,004
The trick is to find rewards that

384
00:12:21,004 --> 00:12:21,921
don't cost much.

385
00:12:22,101 --> 00:12:23,884
A walk, a long call with a

386
00:12:23,884 --> 00:12:26,558
friend, a favorite meal cooked at home,

387
00:12:26,558 --> 00:12:27,929
an hour with a book.

388
00:12:28,109 --> 00:12:31,047
Save the bigger rewards for real milestones.

389
00:12:31,227 --> 00:12:32,812
Try this: for one week,

390
00:12:32,812 --> 00:12:35,362
notice every time you think I deserve

391
00:12:35,362 --> 00:12:36,672
this before buying.

392
00:12:36,852 --> 00:12:37,688
Just count them.

393
00:12:37,868 --> 00:12:40,178
The number alone can change your behavior.

394
00:12:40,528 --> 00:12:41,816
Habit number twelve.

395
00:12:41,996 --> 00:12:43,796
Spending to keep up with friends.

396
00:12:44,146 --> 00:12:46,616
Group dinners, weekend trips,

397
00:12:46,616 --> 00:12:48,916
bachelor parties, concerts,

398
00:12:48,916 --> 00:12:50,194
birthday gifts.

399
00:12:50,374 --> 00:12:52,092
Saying no feels awkward,

400
00:12:52,092 --> 00:12:54,239
so many people say yes and put

401
00:12:54,239 --> 00:12:55,169
it on a card.

402
00:12:55,349 --> 00:12:58,398
Surveys from places like Bankrate and Credit

403
00:12:58,398 --> 00:13:01,100
Karma have found that many adults admit

404
00:13:01,100 --> 00:13:03,871
to overspending to keep up with friends.

405
00:13:04,221 --> 00:13:06,067
Real friends usually understand.

406
00:13:06,247 --> 00:13:08,744
Most people are relieved when someone else

407
00:13:08,744 --> 00:13:10,171
suggests a cheaper plan.

408
00:13:10,351 --> 00:13:12,419
Try this: be the one who suggests

409
00:13:12,419 --> 00:13:13,797
the potluck, the hike,

410
00:13:13,797 --> 00:13:15,239
the game night at home.

411
00:13:15,419 --> 00:13:17,408
And when you can't afford something,

412
00:13:17,408 --> 00:13:18,182
say it simply.

413
00:13:18,362 --> 00:13:20,280
That's not in my budget this month,

414
00:13:20,280 --> 00:13:22,089
but let's do something next week.

415
00:13:22,269 --> 00:13:24,344
You'll be surprised how many people feel

416
00:13:24,344 --> 00:13:24,811
the same.

417
00:13:25,161 --> 00:13:26,600
Habit number thirteen.

418
00:13:26,780 --> 00:13:29,149
Always choosing today over tomorrow.

419
00:13:29,499 --> 00:13:31,925
Economists call it present bias.

420
00:13:32,105 --> 00:13:34,078
We value a reward today much more

421
00:13:34,078 --> 00:13:35,693
than a bigger reward later.

422
00:13:35,873 --> 00:13:38,222
That's why saving for retirement feels so

423
00:13:38,222 --> 00:13:38,508
hard.

424
00:13:38,688 --> 00:13:40,209
Retirement is far away,

425
00:13:40,209 --> 00:13:42,392
and the new jacket is right here.

426
00:13:42,572 --> 00:13:44,996
Your future self feels almost like a

427
00:13:44,996 --> 00:13:45,602
stranger.

428
00:13:45,952 --> 00:13:48,604
Researchers have found that helping people picture

429
00:13:48,604 --> 00:13:50,621
their future selves can make them save

430
00:13:50,621 --> 00:13:50,886
more.

431
00:13:51,066 --> 00:13:53,253
Try this: write a short letter from

432
00:13:53,253 --> 00:13:54,752
yourself at age seventy,

433
00:13:54,752 --> 00:13:57,126
thanking you for what you start today.

434
00:13:57,306 --> 00:13:58,291
It sounds silly,

435
00:13:58,291 --> 00:14:00,383
but it makes the future feel real.

436
00:14:00,563 --> 00:14:03,328
Then set up one small automatic transfer

437
00:14:03,328 --> 00:14:04,986
to a retirement account.

438
00:14:05,337 --> 00:14:06,846
Habit number fourteen.

439
00:14:07,026 --> 00:14:08,500
Not looking at your accounts.

440
00:14:08,851 --> 00:14:11,503
Researchers have documented what they call the

441
00:14:11,503 --> 00:14:12,368
ostrich effect.

442
00:14:12,548 --> 00:14:15,068
People check their accounts less often when

443
00:14:15,068 --> 00:14:16,298
they expect bad news.

444
00:14:16,478 --> 00:14:18,162
It feels better in the moment,

445
00:14:18,162 --> 00:14:19,845
but problems grow in the dark.

446
00:14:20,025 --> 00:14:21,013
Fees pile up,

447
00:14:21,013 --> 00:14:22,607
fraud goes unnoticed,

448
00:14:22,607 --> 00:14:24,658
and debt keeps compounding.

449
00:14:25,008 --> 00:14:27,666
Avoiding money is usually about feelings,

450
00:14:27,666 --> 00:14:29,093
like shame or anxiety,

451
00:14:29,093 --> 00:14:30,325
not about laziness.

452
00:14:30,505 --> 00:14:32,523
The fix is to make looking small

453
00:14:32,523 --> 00:14:33,280
and regular.

454
00:14:33,460 --> 00:14:35,590
Try this: set a ten minute money

455
00:14:35,590 --> 00:14:36,722
date once a week.

456
00:14:36,902 --> 00:14:38,494
Same day, same time,

457
00:14:38,494 --> 00:14:40,165
with a coffee or tea.

458
00:14:40,345 --> 00:14:41,439
Open every account,

459
00:14:41,439 --> 00:14:42,591
look at the balance,

460
00:14:42,591 --> 00:14:44,147
check for anything strange,

461
00:14:44,147 --> 00:14:44,896
and close it.

462
00:14:45,076 --> 00:14:45,563
That's it.

463
00:14:45,914 --> 00:14:46,935
Quick break.

464
00:14:47,115 --> 00:14:48,927
If these habits sound familiar,

465
00:14:48,927 --> 00:14:49,920
you're not alone,

466
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:51,966
and that's exactly why I make these

467
00:14:51,966 --> 00:14:52,375
videos.

468
00:14:52,555 --> 00:14:54,851
If you want more breakdowns like this,

469
00:14:54,851 --> 00:14:56,665
subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

470
00:14:56,845 --> 00:14:58,620
It's free and it really helps the

471
00:14:58,620 --> 00:14:59,050
channel.

472
00:14:59,230 --> 00:15:01,372
Okay, back to the psychology,

473
00:15:01,372 --> 00:15:02,702
and habit fifteen.

474
00:15:03,052 --> 00:15:04,422
Habit number fifteen.

475
00:15:04,602 --> 00:15:06,424
Believing it's too late to start.

476
00:15:06,775 --> 00:15:08,554
People in their thirties think they missed

477
00:15:08,554 --> 00:15:09,189
their twenties.

478
00:15:09,369 --> 00:15:11,429
People in their fifties think they missed

479
00:15:11,429 --> 00:15:11,982
everything.

480
00:15:12,162 --> 00:15:13,810
So they don't start at all.

481
00:15:13,990 --> 00:15:15,430
But time works forward.

482
00:15:15,610 --> 00:15:17,461
The best day to start was years

483
00:15:17,461 --> 00:15:17,700
ago.

484
00:15:17,880 --> 00:15:19,610
The second best day is today.

485
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:21,248
Here's encouraging math.

486
00:15:21,428 --> 00:15:23,588
Someone who starts at forty five and

487
00:15:23,588 --> 00:15:25,927
invests five hundred dollars a month at

488
00:15:25,927 --> 00:15:28,507
a seven percent average yearly return could

489
00:15:28,507 --> 00:15:31,206
have roughly four hundred thousand dollars by

490
00:15:31,206 --> 00:15:31,866
sixty five.

491
00:15:32,046 --> 00:15:34,165
Not the same as starting at twenty

492
00:15:34,165 --> 00:15:36,098
five, but far better than zero.

493
00:15:36,278 --> 00:15:38,364
Try this: replace the thought it's too

494
00:15:38,364 --> 00:15:40,176
late with the question what can I

495
00:15:40,176 --> 00:15:41,548
do in the next ten years?

496
00:15:41,899 --> 00:15:43,443
Habit number sixteen.

497
00:15:43,623 --> 00:15:46,514
Believing a bigger paycheck will fix everything.

498
00:15:46,864 --> 00:15:48,326
More income helps,

499
00:15:48,326 --> 00:15:49,220
absolutely.

500
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:51,298
But if the habits don't change,

501
00:15:51,298 --> 00:15:53,441
spending tends to rise to match the

502
00:15:53,441 --> 00:15:54,114
new income.

503
00:15:54,294 --> 00:15:57,139
Economists call this lifestyle inflation.

504
00:15:57,319 --> 00:15:59,757
Plenty of high earners live paycheck to

505
00:15:59,757 --> 00:16:02,571
paycheck, and plenty of average earners build

506
00:16:02,571 --> 00:16:03,321
real wealth.

507
00:16:03,671 --> 00:16:04,832
The test is simple.

508
00:16:05,012 --> 00:16:07,047
If you can't save anything at your

509
00:16:07,047 --> 00:16:09,680
current income, a raise alone probably won't

510
00:16:09,680 --> 00:16:10,399
change that.

511
00:16:10,579 --> 00:16:12,285
The habit has to come first.

512
00:16:12,465 --> 00:16:15,640
Try this: practice saving even five percent

513
00:16:15,640 --> 00:16:17,191
of what you earn now.

514
00:16:17,371 --> 00:16:18,631
When the raise comes,

515
00:16:18,631 --> 00:16:20,792
keep your lifestyle the same for six

516
00:16:20,792 --> 00:16:22,653
months and save the difference.

517
00:16:23,003 --> 00:16:24,605
Habit number seventeen.

518
00:16:24,785 --> 00:16:26,747
Believing wealth is for other people.

519
00:16:27,097 --> 00:16:29,512
Some people grow up hearing that rich

520
00:16:29,512 --> 00:16:30,688
people are greedy,

521
00:16:30,688 --> 00:16:32,581
that money is the root of all

522
00:16:32,581 --> 00:16:34,801
evil, or that people like us don't

523
00:16:34,801 --> 00:16:35,258
invest.

524
00:16:35,438 --> 00:16:37,925
Those beliefs can quietly stop you from

525
00:16:37,925 --> 00:16:39,837
opening an investment account,

526
00:16:39,837 --> 00:16:41,049
asking for a raise,

527
00:16:41,049 --> 00:16:42,834
or learning how money works.

528
00:16:43,184 --> 00:16:46,359
Stanley's research found that many American millionaires

529
00:16:46,359 --> 00:16:48,060
were first generation wealthy.

530
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:49,389
They didn't inherit it.

531
00:16:49,569 --> 00:16:50,931
They were teachers,

532
00:16:50,931 --> 00:16:53,296
engineers, small business owners.

533
00:16:53,476 --> 00:16:55,879
Try this: write down three beliefs about

534
00:16:55,879 --> 00:16:57,261
money you grew up with.

535
00:16:57,441 --> 00:16:58,740
Next to each one,

536
00:16:58,740 --> 00:17:00,803
ask: is this actually true,

537
00:17:00,803 --> 00:17:02,561
or is it just familiar?

538
00:17:02,911 --> 00:17:04,742
Let's go one level deeper on the

539
00:17:04,742 --> 00:17:07,661
psychology, because understanding this takes away a

540
00:17:07,661 --> 00:17:08,634
lot of the guilt.

541
00:17:08,814 --> 00:17:11,062
Spending gives your brain a reward right

542
00:17:11,062 --> 00:17:11,287
now.

543
00:17:11,467 --> 00:17:13,986
Saving gives you a reward years from

544
00:17:13,986 --> 00:17:16,085
now, in a future you can't see

545
00:17:16,085 --> 00:17:16,645
or feel.

546
00:17:16,825 --> 00:17:19,424
Studies of decision making show that people

547
00:17:19,424 --> 00:17:21,842
tend to discount future rewards steeply,

548
00:17:21,842 --> 00:17:23,655
which is a fancy way of saying

549
00:17:23,655 --> 00:17:25,892
a dollar today feels much bigger than

550
00:17:25,892 --> 00:17:26,798
a dollar later.

551
00:17:27,149 --> 00:17:28,085
On top of that,

552
00:17:28,085 --> 00:17:30,770
modern shopping is designed to remove every

553
00:17:30,770 --> 00:17:31,769
bit of friction.

554
00:17:31,949 --> 00:17:33,446
One click checkout,

555
00:17:33,446 --> 00:17:35,338
saved cards, tap to pay,

556
00:17:35,338 --> 00:17:36,756
buy now pay later.

557
00:17:36,936 --> 00:17:39,620
Researchers have found that people tend to

558
00:17:39,620 --> 00:17:42,304
spend more when paying feels less painful,

559
00:17:42,304 --> 00:17:44,796
for example with cards instead of cash.

560
00:17:44,976 --> 00:17:46,556
When paying doesn't hurt,

561
00:17:46,556 --> 00:17:48,830
spending grows without you noticing.

562
00:17:49,181 --> 00:17:51,665
So the solution isn't to feel more

563
00:17:51,665 --> 00:17:52,176
guilty.

564
00:17:52,356 --> 00:17:54,028
It's to flip the friction.

565
00:17:54,208 --> 00:17:56,454
Make spending a little harder and saving

566
00:17:56,454 --> 00:17:57,633
completely automatic.

567
00:17:57,813 --> 00:18:00,564
Delete saved cards from shopping sites.

568
00:18:00,744 --> 00:18:02,416
Turn off one click buying.

569
00:18:02,596 --> 00:18:04,651
Take shopping apps off your home screen.

570
00:18:04,831 --> 00:18:06,639
And move savings automatically,

571
00:18:06,639 --> 00:18:09,088
so the good choice happens without needing

572
00:18:09,088 --> 00:18:10,079
willpower at all.

573
00:18:10,429 --> 00:18:11,357
That's angle two.

574
00:18:11,537 --> 00:18:13,381
Mental accounting, rewards,

575
00:18:13,381 --> 00:18:15,292
peer pressure, present bias,

576
00:18:15,292 --> 00:18:18,364
avoidance, and the stories we tell ourselves.

577
00:18:18,544 --> 00:18:19,693
Now angle three.

578
00:18:19,873 --> 00:18:20,582
The playbook.

579
00:18:20,762 --> 00:18:22,897
These are the systems that are missing

580
00:18:22,897 --> 00:18:24,303
in most broke households,

581
00:18:24,303 --> 00:18:26,157
and the good news is they're easy

582
00:18:26,157 --> 00:18:26,776
to install.

583
00:18:27,126 --> 00:18:28,844
Willpower runs out.

584
00:18:29,024 --> 00:18:30,034
Systems don't.

585
00:18:30,214 --> 00:18:32,130
The people who stay on track with

586
00:18:32,130 --> 00:18:35,090
money usually aren't more disciplined than everyone

587
00:18:35,090 --> 00:18:35,380
else.

588
00:18:35,560 --> 00:18:37,592
They've just built a few simple systems

589
00:18:37,592 --> 00:18:39,728
so the right thing happens automatically.

590
00:18:39,908 --> 00:18:41,831
The last eight habits on our list

591
00:18:41,831 --> 00:18:42,997
are missing systems.

592
00:18:43,347 --> 00:18:44,809
Habit number eighteen.

593
00:18:44,989 --> 00:18:47,126
Living without an emergency fund.

594
00:18:47,476 --> 00:18:49,075
Without a cash cushion,

595
00:18:49,075 --> 00:18:50,187
every flat tire,

596
00:18:50,187 --> 00:18:52,760
dentist visit or broken phone goes on

597
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:53,733
a credit card.

598
00:18:53,913 --> 00:18:56,412
Then the interest makes the surprise even

599
00:18:56,412 --> 00:18:57,327
more expensive.

600
00:18:57,507 --> 00:18:59,419
It's one of the main ways people

601
00:18:59,419 --> 00:19:01,092
get stuck in a cycle of debt

602
00:19:01,092 --> 00:19:02,406
that never quite ends.

603
00:19:02,756 --> 00:19:05,300
A common starting goal is one thousand

604
00:19:05,300 --> 00:19:07,711
dollars, then one month of expenses,

605
00:19:07,711 --> 00:19:09,385
then three to six months.

606
00:19:09,565 --> 00:19:12,312
Try this: open a separate savings account

607
00:19:12,312 --> 00:19:13,719
just for emergencies,

608
00:19:13,719 --> 00:19:15,730
with a name like do not touch.

609
00:19:15,910 --> 00:19:18,380
Set an automatic transfer of even twenty

610
00:19:18,380 --> 00:19:19,985
five dollars every payday.

611
00:19:20,165 --> 00:19:23,543
Small and automatic beats big and someday.

612
00:19:23,894 --> 00:19:25,310
Habit number nineteen.

613
00:19:25,490 --> 00:19:27,497
Saving whatever is left at the end

614
00:19:27,497 --> 00:19:28,265
of the month.

615
00:19:28,615 --> 00:19:29,574
For most people,

616
00:19:29,574 --> 00:19:31,614
there's nothing left at the end of

617
00:19:31,614 --> 00:19:32,214
the month.

618
00:19:32,394 --> 00:19:35,563
Spending expands to fill whatever is available.

619
00:19:35,743 --> 00:19:38,448
That's why saving last almost never works.

620
00:19:38,799 --> 00:19:41,039
The fix is to pay yourself first.

621
00:19:41,219 --> 00:19:43,952
On payday, before you spend anything,

622
00:19:43,952 --> 00:19:47,275
a set amount moves automatically into savings

623
00:19:47,275 --> 00:19:48,383
or investments.

624
00:19:48,563 --> 00:19:50,281
You then live on what remains.

625
00:19:50,461 --> 00:19:52,786
Try this: set up an automatic transfer

626
00:19:52,786 --> 00:19:54,315
for the day after payday.

627
00:19:54,495 --> 00:19:56,622
Start with five percent of your take

628
00:19:56,622 --> 00:19:57,154
home pay.

629
00:19:57,334 --> 00:19:59,518
Raise it by one percent every few

630
00:19:59,518 --> 00:19:59,981
months.

631
00:20:00,161 --> 00:20:01,450
You'll barely notice.

632
00:20:01,800 --> 00:20:03,065
Habit number twenty.

633
00:20:03,245 --> 00:20:05,312
Not taking the full employer match.

634
00:20:05,662 --> 00:20:08,012
Many employers match part of what you

635
00:20:08,012 --> 00:20:09,600
put into a four oh one k,

636
00:20:09,600 --> 00:20:12,141
for example fifty cents for every dollar

637
00:20:12,141 --> 00:20:14,110
up to a certain percent of your

638
00:20:14,110 --> 00:20:14,555
salary.

639
00:20:14,735 --> 00:20:16,578
If you don't contribute enough to get

640
00:20:16,578 --> 00:20:17,326
the full match,

641
00:20:17,326 --> 00:20:19,020
you're leaving part of your pay on

642
00:20:19,020 --> 00:20:19,518
the table.

643
00:20:19,868 --> 00:20:21,504
It's one of the few places in

644
00:20:21,504 --> 00:20:23,817
personal finance where you get an instant

645
00:20:23,817 --> 00:20:25,510
return just for participating.

646
00:20:25,690 --> 00:20:28,809
Try this: log into your benefits portal

647
00:20:28,809 --> 00:20:30,488
this week, or ask HR,

648
00:20:30,488 --> 00:20:33,287
and find out exactly what the match

649
00:20:33,287 --> 00:20:33,527
is.

650
00:20:33,707 --> 00:20:35,772
Then make sure your contribution is at

651
00:20:35,772 --> 00:20:37,456
least high enough to get all of

652
00:20:37,456 --> 00:20:37,619
it.

653
00:20:37,970 --> 00:20:39,398
Habit number twenty one.

654
00:20:39,578 --> 00:20:41,040
Never investing at all.

655
00:20:41,391 --> 00:20:42,900
Saving keeps money safe.

656
00:20:43,080 --> 00:20:44,647
Investing helps it grow.

657
00:20:44,827 --> 00:20:46,008
Over long periods,

658
00:20:46,008 --> 00:20:49,551
broad stock market investments have historically grown

659
00:20:49,551 --> 00:20:52,372
faster than savings accounts and inflation,

660
00:20:52,372 --> 00:20:54,669
though with ups and downs along the

661
00:20:54,669 --> 00:20:56,112
way and no guarantees.

662
00:20:56,292 --> 00:20:58,450
People who never invest miss out on

663
00:20:58,450 --> 00:20:59,868
compounding completely.

664
00:21:00,218 --> 00:21:01,576
You don't need to pick stocks.

665
00:21:01,756 --> 00:21:03,328
Many people use broad,

666
00:21:03,328 --> 00:21:06,258
low cost index funds through a retirement

667
00:21:06,258 --> 00:21:06,829
account.

668
00:21:07,009 --> 00:21:09,560
Try this: once your emergency fund is

669
00:21:09,560 --> 00:21:12,249
started and high interest debt is under

670
00:21:12,249 --> 00:21:15,213
control, open a retirement account and make

671
00:21:15,213 --> 00:21:18,109
one small automatic investment each month.

672
00:21:18,289 --> 00:21:20,685
Learning by doing beats waiting until you

673
00:21:20,685 --> 00:21:21,621
know everything.

674
00:21:21,971 --> 00:21:23,375
Habit number twenty two.

675
00:21:23,555 --> 00:21:25,541
Having no written money goals.

676
00:21:25,891 --> 00:21:26,825
Without a goal,

677
00:21:26,825 --> 00:21:29,629
every purchase competes only with the moment.

678
00:21:29,809 --> 00:21:30,527
With a goal,

679
00:21:30,527 --> 00:21:33,461
every purchase competes with something you really

680
00:21:33,461 --> 00:21:34,599
want, like a house,

681
00:21:34,599 --> 00:21:35,676
freedom from debt,

682
00:21:35,676 --> 00:21:37,054
or an early retirement.

683
00:21:37,234 --> 00:21:38,882
Goals give saving a reason.

684
00:21:39,233 --> 00:21:40,579
Make goals specific.

685
00:21:40,759 --> 00:21:41,716
Not save more,

686
00:21:41,716 --> 00:21:44,174
but save six thousand dollars for an

687
00:21:44,174 --> 00:21:46,019
emergency fund by December.

688
00:21:46,199 --> 00:21:49,090
Try this: write down three money goals,

689
00:21:49,090 --> 00:21:50,425
one for this year,

690
00:21:50,425 --> 00:21:51,833
one for five years,

691
00:21:51,833 --> 00:21:53,687
and one for twenty years.

692
00:21:53,867 --> 00:21:55,506
Put a number and a date on

693
00:21:55,506 --> 00:21:56,073
each one.

694
00:21:56,253 --> 00:21:58,192
Keep the list where you'll see it.

695
00:21:58,542 --> 00:21:59,877
Habit number twenty three.

696
00:22:00,057 --> 00:22:01,299
Never negotiating.

697
00:22:01,648 --> 00:22:04,048
Salary, rent, insurance,

698
00:22:04,048 --> 00:22:06,448
phone plans, car prices,

699
00:22:06,448 --> 00:22:07,848
medical bills.

700
00:22:08,028 --> 00:22:10,254
Many of these are more flexible than

701
00:22:10,254 --> 00:22:11,058
people think.

702
00:22:11,238 --> 00:22:13,314
But most people accept the first number

703
00:22:13,314 --> 00:22:14,060
they're given.

704
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:15,192
Over a career,

705
00:22:15,192 --> 00:22:17,778
not negotiating salary can cost a lot,

706
00:22:17,778 --> 00:22:20,364
because every future raise is built on

707
00:22:20,364 --> 00:22:22,065
top of your starting pay.

708
00:22:22,414 --> 00:22:24,864
Negotiating doesn't have to be aggressive.

709
00:22:25,044 --> 00:22:27,436
It's research plus a polite question.

710
00:22:27,616 --> 00:22:29,045
Try this: once a year,

711
00:22:29,045 --> 00:22:31,839
call your insurance and phone providers and

712
00:22:31,839 --> 00:22:33,723
ask if there's a better rate.

713
00:22:33,903 --> 00:22:35,561
And before your next review,

714
00:22:35,561 --> 00:22:37,515
look up salary data for your role

715
00:22:37,515 --> 00:22:39,054
so you can ask for a raise

716
00:22:39,054 --> 00:22:40,416
with numbers, not hope.

717
00:22:40,765 --> 00:22:42,240
Habit number twenty four.

718
00:22:42,420 --> 00:22:44,220
Skipping basic protection.

719
00:22:44,569 --> 00:22:47,851
Health insurance, renters or homeowners insurance,

720
00:22:47,851 --> 00:22:49,229
enough car insurance,

721
00:22:49,229 --> 00:22:51,723
and life insurance if people depend on

722
00:22:51,723 --> 00:22:52,511
your income.

723
00:22:52,691 --> 00:22:55,426
One uninsured accident or illness can wipe

724
00:22:55,426 --> 00:22:56,859
out years of progress.

725
00:22:57,039 --> 00:22:59,802
Medical bills are a common reason families

726
00:22:59,802 --> 00:23:00,789
fall into debt.

727
00:23:01,138 --> 00:23:03,487
Insurance is boring until the day you

728
00:23:03,487 --> 00:23:03,995
need it.

729
00:23:04,175 --> 00:23:06,303
The goal isn't to insure everything,

730
00:23:06,303 --> 00:23:08,669
but to protect against the disasters you

731
00:23:08,669 --> 00:23:09,852
could never pay for.

732
00:23:10,032 --> 00:23:12,110
Try this: make a one page list

733
00:23:12,110 --> 00:23:13,841
of every policy you have,

734
00:23:13,841 --> 00:23:16,127
what it covers and what it costs.

735
00:23:16,307 --> 00:23:17,210
Look for gaps,

736
00:23:17,210 --> 00:23:19,337
and look for overlap you can cut.

737
00:23:19,687 --> 00:23:21,290
And habit number twenty five.

738
00:23:21,470 --> 00:23:22,921
Trying to do it all alone.

739
00:23:23,270 --> 00:23:25,358
Money is still a taboo topic in

740
00:23:25,358 --> 00:23:26,301
many families.

741
00:23:26,481 --> 00:23:28,894
People don't talk about salaries,

742
00:23:28,894 --> 00:23:30,138
debt or mistakes.

743
00:23:30,318 --> 00:23:32,291
So everyone struggles in private,

744
00:23:32,291 --> 00:23:35,101
repeating the same mistakes their parents made.

745
00:23:35,281 --> 00:23:37,420
Couples who never talk about money often

746
00:23:37,420 --> 00:23:38,543
fight about it later.

747
00:23:38,893 --> 00:23:40,252
Find your money team.

748
00:23:40,432 --> 00:23:42,117
That could be your partner,

749
00:23:42,117 --> 00:23:44,301
a friend working on the same goals,

750
00:23:44,301 --> 00:23:46,423
a free nonprofit credit counselor,

751
00:23:46,423 --> 00:23:48,419
or a fee only financial planner.

752
00:23:48,599 --> 00:23:51,563
Try this: schedule one honest money conversation

753
00:23:51,563 --> 00:23:53,661
this month with someone you trust.

754
00:23:53,841 --> 00:23:56,384
Share one goal and one struggle.

755
00:23:56,564 --> 00:23:58,770
Saying it out loud makes it real.

756
00:23:59,119 --> 00:24:01,014
Let me show you how these habits

757
00:24:01,014 --> 00:24:03,382
play out with two people earning exactly

758
00:24:03,382 --> 00:24:03,914
the same.

759
00:24:04,264 --> 00:24:07,002
Jordan and Taylor both earn sixty thousand

760
00:24:07,002 --> 00:24:07,980
dollars a year.

761
00:24:08,160 --> 00:24:10,008
Jordan has most of the habits on

762
00:24:10,008 --> 00:24:10,586
this list.

763
00:24:10,766 --> 00:24:11,778
A car payment,

764
00:24:11,778 --> 00:24:13,369
a credit card balance,

765
00:24:13,369 --> 00:24:15,105
a pile of subscriptions,

766
00:24:15,105 --> 00:24:16,551
takeout most nights,

767
00:24:16,551 --> 00:24:18,359
and no automatic savings.

768
00:24:18,539 --> 00:24:21,622
Every month, Jordan's account hits nearly zero

769
00:24:21,622 --> 00:24:22,962
right before payday.

770
00:24:23,142 --> 00:24:24,547
Jordan isn't reckless.

771
00:24:24,727 --> 00:24:27,490
Jordan is just running on default settings.

772
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:29,199
Taylor earns the same,

773
00:24:29,199 --> 00:24:31,114
but set up a few systems early.

774
00:24:31,294 --> 00:24:34,030
Automatic transfer to savings the day after

775
00:24:34,030 --> 00:24:34,476
payday.

776
00:24:34,656 --> 00:24:36,142
Full employer match.

777
00:24:36,322 --> 00:24:38,574
A used car paid in cash.

778
00:24:38,754 --> 00:24:40,774
Credit card paid in full.

779
00:24:40,954 --> 00:24:43,032
Groceries planned on Sundays.

780
00:24:43,212 --> 00:24:45,604
Taylor still eats out and travels,

781
00:24:45,604 --> 00:24:46,730
just on purpose.

782
00:24:47,080 --> 00:24:48,149
Ten years later,

783
00:24:48,149 --> 00:24:50,552
Taylor has a full emergency fund and

784
00:24:50,552 --> 00:24:52,955
retirement savings growing steadily.

785
00:24:53,135 --> 00:24:54,917
Jordan has the same income,

786
00:24:54,917 --> 00:24:56,170
the same job title,

787
00:24:56,170 --> 00:24:57,556
and still no cushion,

788
00:24:57,556 --> 00:24:59,997
plus a credit card balance that never

789
00:24:59,997 --> 00:25:01,053
seems to shrink.

790
00:25:01,233 --> 00:25:02,382
Same paycheck.

791
00:25:02,562 --> 00:25:04,327
Completely different life.

792
00:25:04,507 --> 00:25:06,225
The difference was never income.

793
00:25:06,405 --> 00:25:07,404
It was habits.

794
00:25:07,753 --> 00:25:09,257
Fifteen years later,

795
00:25:09,257 --> 00:25:10,911
the gap is even wider.

796
00:25:11,091 --> 00:25:14,090
Taylor's investments are now growing by thousands

797
00:25:14,090 --> 00:25:15,681
of dollars in a good year,

798
00:25:15,681 --> 00:25:17,639
sometimes more than Taylor adds.

799
00:25:17,819 --> 00:25:19,945
Taylor could handle a job loss,

800
00:25:19,945 --> 00:25:21,384
help a family member,

801
00:25:21,384 --> 00:25:23,578
or change careers without panic.

802
00:25:23,758 --> 00:25:26,242
Jordan, meanwhile, feels trapped.

803
00:25:26,422 --> 00:25:28,636
Every raise has been swallowed by a

804
00:25:28,636 --> 00:25:29,395
new payment.

805
00:25:29,575 --> 00:25:31,974
Jordan works just as hard as Taylor,

806
00:25:31,974 --> 00:25:34,173
maybe harder, and has far less to

807
00:25:34,173 --> 00:25:34,973
show for it.

808
00:25:35,323 --> 00:25:37,329
The good news is that Jordan can

809
00:25:37,329 --> 00:25:38,770
still turn this around,

810
00:25:38,770 --> 00:25:39,584
at any point.

811
00:25:39,764 --> 00:25:41,997
Not by becoming a different person,

812
00:25:41,997 --> 00:25:44,293
but by copying Taylor's few systems,

813
00:25:44,293 --> 00:25:45,186
one at a time.

814
00:25:45,366 --> 00:25:46,190
Autopay.

815
00:25:46,370 --> 00:25:47,694
Pay yourself first.

816
00:25:47,874 --> 00:25:49,070
Get the full match.

817
00:25:49,250 --> 00:25:50,411
Pay off the card.

818
00:25:50,591 --> 00:25:51,438
Plan dinners.

819
00:25:51,618 --> 00:25:52,826
Each one is small.

820
00:25:53,006 --> 00:25:55,038
Together, they rewrite the story.

821
00:25:55,387 --> 00:25:57,249
Breaking a habit is much easier when

822
00:25:57,249 --> 00:25:58,801
you replace it with something.

823
00:25:58,981 --> 00:26:01,024
So here are the swaps that people

824
00:26:01,024 --> 00:26:02,882
who build wealth tend to make.

825
00:26:03,231 --> 00:26:03,963
Swap one.

826
00:26:04,143 --> 00:26:06,814
Instead of checking social media when you're

827
00:26:06,814 --> 00:26:09,182
bored, check your accounts once a week.

828
00:26:09,362 --> 00:26:10,176
It sounds dull,

829
00:26:10,176 --> 00:26:12,022
but people who look at their money

830
00:26:12,022 --> 00:26:14,412
regularly tend to make better decisions with

831
00:26:14,412 --> 00:26:14,574
it.

832
00:26:14,754 --> 00:26:16,080
You catch fraud early,

833
00:26:16,080 --> 00:26:17,345
you notice the leaks,

834
00:26:17,345 --> 00:26:18,912
and you see your progress,

835
00:26:18,912 --> 00:26:20,478
which keeps you motivated.

836
00:26:20,828 --> 00:26:21,757
Swap two.

837
00:26:21,937 --> 00:26:24,594
Instead of the monthly payment question,

838
00:26:24,594 --> 00:26:26,454
ask the total cost question.

839
00:26:26,634 --> 00:26:28,669
What will this cost me in total,

840
00:26:28,669 --> 00:26:30,577
including interest, insurance,

841
00:26:30,577 --> 00:26:32,613
maintenance and everything else?

842
00:26:32,793 --> 00:26:33,896
Swap three.

843
00:26:34,076 --> 00:26:35,927
Instead of buying on impulse,

844
00:26:35,927 --> 00:26:37,141
use a waiting rule.

845
00:26:37,321 --> 00:26:39,700
Twenty four hours for small things,

846
00:26:39,700 --> 00:26:41,535
thirty days for big things.

847
00:26:41,715 --> 00:26:43,039
Most wants fade.

848
00:26:43,219 --> 00:26:45,407
The ones that remain are probably worth

849
00:26:45,407 --> 00:26:45,575
it.

850
00:26:45,925 --> 00:26:46,831
Swap four.

851
00:26:47,011 --> 00:26:49,043
Instead of spending a windfall,

852
00:26:49,043 --> 00:26:50,355
split it in advance.

853
00:26:50,535 --> 00:26:51,672
Swap five.

854
00:26:51,852 --> 00:26:53,953
Instead of saving what's left,

855
00:26:53,953 --> 00:26:56,264
save first and spend what's left.

856
00:26:56,444 --> 00:26:57,501
Swap six.

857
00:26:57,681 --> 00:26:59,519
Instead of avoiding money talk,

858
00:26:59,519 --> 00:27:01,771
have a regular money meeting with your

859
00:27:01,771 --> 00:27:03,254
partner or with yourself.

860
00:27:03,434 --> 00:27:04,953
Same day each month,

861
00:27:04,953 --> 00:27:06,092
same questions.

862
00:27:06,272 --> 00:27:07,170
What came in,

863
00:27:07,170 --> 00:27:08,136
what went out,

864
00:27:08,136 --> 00:27:09,241
are we on track,

865
00:27:09,241 --> 00:27:11,450
and what's one thing to improve?

866
00:27:11,800 --> 00:27:12,892
Swap seven.

867
00:27:13,072 --> 00:27:16,117
Instead of comparing yourself with people online,

868
00:27:16,117 --> 00:27:18,354
compare yourself with your own past.

869
00:27:18,534 --> 00:27:20,262
Are you better off than you were

870
00:27:20,262 --> 00:27:20,856
a year ago?

871
00:27:21,036 --> 00:27:22,491
Do you have more saved,

872
00:27:22,491 --> 00:27:24,136
less debt, a clearer plan?

873
00:27:24,316 --> 00:27:26,444
That's the only comparison that matters,

874
00:27:26,444 --> 00:27:28,252
and it's one you can actually win.

875
00:27:28,432 --> 00:27:29,303
Swap eight.

876
00:27:29,483 --> 00:27:31,864
Instead of waiting for the perfect moment,

877
00:27:31,864 --> 00:27:34,359
start with the smallest possible step today.

878
00:27:34,708 --> 00:27:36,705
Here's a simple four week reset.

879
00:27:36,885 --> 00:27:38,221
Week one, look.

880
00:27:38,401 --> 00:27:39,764
Track every purchase,

881
00:27:39,764 --> 00:27:42,492
list every payment and every subscription,

882
00:27:42,492 --> 00:27:44,310
and check your savings rate.

883
00:27:44,490 --> 00:27:46,197
Week two, cut.

884
00:27:46,377 --> 00:27:47,977
Cancel what you don't use,

885
00:27:47,977 --> 00:27:49,208
call to remove fees,

886
00:27:49,208 --> 00:27:50,870
and ask for one lower rate.

887
00:27:51,219 --> 00:27:52,822
Week three, automate.

888
00:27:53,002 --> 00:27:54,559
Set up autopay for bills,

889
00:27:54,559 --> 00:27:57,051
an automatic transfer to savings the day

890
00:27:57,051 --> 00:27:59,668
after payday, and make sure you're getting

891
00:27:59,668 --> 00:28:01,163
the full employer match.

892
00:28:01,343 --> 00:28:02,795
Week four, aim.

893
00:28:02,975 --> 00:28:05,281
Write your three goals with numbers and

894
00:28:05,281 --> 00:28:07,469
dates, and share them with one person

895
00:28:07,469 --> 00:28:08,060
you trust.

896
00:28:08,240 --> 00:28:09,180
Four weeks.

897
00:28:09,360 --> 00:28:11,055
That's all it takes to start turning

898
00:28:11,055 --> 00:28:11,996
these habits around.

899
00:28:12,345 --> 00:28:14,028
Where you start depends a bit on

900
00:28:14,028 --> 00:28:15,027
your stage of life.

901
00:28:15,207 --> 00:28:16,337
In your twenties,

902
00:28:16,337 --> 00:28:19,062
the most valuable habits are the systems.

903
00:28:19,242 --> 00:28:22,272
Automatic saving, getting the full employer match,

904
00:28:22,272 --> 00:28:24,211
and avoiding high interest debt.

905
00:28:24,391 --> 00:28:26,210
Time is your biggest asset,

906
00:28:26,210 --> 00:28:28,904
so even small amounts invested early can

907
00:28:28,904 --> 00:28:31,194
grow a lot over the decades ahead.

908
00:28:31,544 --> 00:28:33,496
In your thirties and forties,

909
00:28:33,496 --> 00:28:34,842
life gets expensive.

910
00:28:35,022 --> 00:28:36,915
Kids, housing, cars,

911
00:28:36,915 --> 00:28:39,283
maybe caring for parents.

912
00:28:39,463 --> 00:28:42,400
This is when lifestyle inflation and monthly

913
00:28:42,400 --> 00:28:44,269
payments do the most damage.

914
00:28:44,449 --> 00:28:47,069
Focus on keeping fixed costs under control

915
00:28:47,069 --> 00:28:49,564
and raising your savings rate every time

916
00:28:49,564 --> 00:28:50,811
your income goes up.

917
00:28:51,161 --> 00:28:52,839
In your fifties and beyond,

918
00:28:52,839 --> 00:28:55,014
the focus shifts to catching up and

919
00:28:55,014 --> 00:28:56,816
protecting what you've built.

920
00:28:56,996 --> 00:29:00,262
Many retirement accounts allow extra catch up

921
00:29:00,262 --> 00:29:02,149
contributions after fifty,

922
00:29:02,149 --> 00:29:04,472
so check the current IRS limits.

923
00:29:04,652 --> 00:29:07,151
And the psychology habits matter more than

924
00:29:07,151 --> 00:29:10,066
ever, especially avoiding panic and not believing

925
00:29:10,066 --> 00:29:10,899
it's too late.

926
00:29:11,248 --> 00:29:12,073
Question one.

927
00:29:12,253 --> 00:29:13,936
Which habit should I fix first?

928
00:29:14,116 --> 00:29:15,974
Start with the most expensive one.

929
00:29:16,154 --> 00:29:17,031
For most people,

930
00:29:17,031 --> 00:29:19,114
that's high interest credit card debt.

931
00:29:19,294 --> 00:29:22,017
Second, start an emergency fund so new

932
00:29:22,017 --> 00:29:24,310
surprises don't become new debt.

933
00:29:24,490 --> 00:29:27,125
Everything else gets easier after those two.

934
00:29:27,475 --> 00:29:28,288
Question two.

935
00:29:28,468 --> 00:29:30,504
What if my income really is too

936
00:29:30,504 --> 00:29:30,767
low?

937
00:29:30,947 --> 00:29:33,687
Then the focus shifts toward income,

938
00:29:33,687 --> 00:29:35,819
like skills, certifications,

939
00:29:35,819 --> 00:29:37,341
a better paying job,

940
00:29:37,341 --> 00:29:38,635
or a side income,

941
00:29:38,635 --> 00:29:41,756
while keeping the basic systems in place.

942
00:29:41,936 --> 00:29:44,228
Even saving a very small amount builds

943
00:29:44,228 --> 00:29:46,399
the habit that will matter when your

944
00:29:46,399 --> 00:29:47,183
income grows.

945
00:29:47,533 --> 00:29:48,578
Question three.

946
00:29:48,758 --> 00:29:50,429
How long does it take to change

947
00:29:50,429 --> 00:29:50,860
a habit?

948
00:29:51,040 --> 00:29:54,125
Research on habit formation suggests it varies

949
00:29:54,125 --> 00:29:56,205
a lot from person to person and

950
00:29:56,205 --> 00:29:57,546
from habit to habit,

951
00:29:57,546 --> 00:29:59,492
often taking weeks to months.

952
00:29:59,672 --> 00:30:02,017
That's why automation is so powerful.

953
00:30:02,197 --> 00:30:04,800
An automatic transfer doesn't need to become

954
00:30:04,800 --> 00:30:05,274
a habit.

955
00:30:05,454 --> 00:30:06,533
It just happens.

956
00:30:06,883 --> 00:30:07,812
Question four.

957
00:30:07,992 --> 00:30:09,409
Should I use a budget app?

958
00:30:09,589 --> 00:30:10,616
If it helps,

959
00:30:10,616 --> 00:30:10,959
yes.

960
00:30:11,139 --> 00:30:12,957
But the best budget is the one

961
00:30:12,957 --> 00:30:14,169
you'll actually use.

962
00:30:14,349 --> 00:30:16,122
For some people that's an app,

963
00:30:16,122 --> 00:30:17,600
for others a spreadsheet,

964
00:30:17,600 --> 00:30:18,900
for others a notebook.

965
00:30:19,080 --> 00:30:21,483
What matters is looking at your numbers

966
00:30:21,483 --> 00:30:22,099
regularly.

967
00:30:22,448 --> 00:30:23,470
Question five.

968
00:30:23,650 --> 00:30:25,436
Is it bad to buy coffee every

969
00:30:25,436 --> 00:30:25,682
day?

970
00:30:25,862 --> 00:30:27,457
Not if it's a choice you've made

971
00:30:27,457 --> 00:30:29,101
on purpose and it fits your plan.

972
00:30:29,281 --> 00:30:30,721
The coffee isn't the problem.

973
00:30:30,901 --> 00:30:32,517
Not knowing what you spend is the

974
00:30:32,517 --> 00:30:32,909
problem.

975
00:30:33,089 --> 00:30:34,529
If your bills are paid,

976
00:30:34,529 --> 00:30:37,095
your emergency fund is growing and you're

977
00:30:37,095 --> 00:30:38,848
investing, enjoy the coffee.

978
00:30:39,028 --> 00:30:40,623
If not, it might be one of

979
00:30:40,623 --> 00:30:42,708
many small leaks worth looking at.

980
00:30:43,058 --> 00:30:44,196
Question six.

981
00:30:44,376 --> 00:30:46,365
What if my partner has these habits

982
00:30:46,365 --> 00:30:47,047
and I don't?

983
00:30:47,227 --> 00:30:49,185
Start with shared goals,

984
00:30:49,185 --> 00:30:50,001
not blame.

985
00:30:50,181 --> 00:30:52,141
Talk about what you both want,

986
00:30:52,141 --> 00:30:53,969
then build systems together,

987
00:30:53,969 --> 00:30:56,255
like a joint savings transfer and a

988
00:30:56,255 --> 00:30:58,541
personal allowance for each of you.

989
00:30:58,721 --> 00:31:00,799
Blame rarely changes behavior.

990
00:31:00,979 --> 00:31:02,651
A shared plan often does.

991
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:03,894
Question seven.

992
00:31:04,074 --> 00:31:05,451
Do I need to cut all fun

993
00:31:05,451 --> 00:31:05,967
spending?

994
00:31:06,147 --> 00:31:06,333
No.

995
00:31:06,513 --> 00:31:08,684
Cutting everything usually backfires.

996
00:31:08,864 --> 00:31:09,690
Plan for fun,

997
00:31:09,690 --> 00:31:11,153
give it a fixed amount,

998
00:31:11,153 --> 00:31:12,869
and spend it without guilt.

999
00:31:13,049 --> 00:31:14,769
The goal is a life you enjoy

1000
00:31:14,769 --> 00:31:16,489
that also moves you forward,

1001
00:31:16,489 --> 00:31:18,578
not a life of constant denial that

1002
00:31:18,578 --> 00:31:19,806
you eventually quit.

1003
00:31:20,156 --> 00:31:20,981
Quick recap.

1004
00:31:21,161 --> 00:31:22,856
Angle one, the numbers.

1005
00:31:23,036 --> 00:31:24,717
Not tracking spending,

1006
00:31:24,717 --> 00:31:26,169
carrying a balance,

1007
00:31:26,169 --> 00:31:27,468
paying bank fees,

1008
00:31:27,468 --> 00:31:29,684
thinking in monthly payments,

1009
00:31:29,684 --> 00:31:33,199
ignoring subscriptions, eating out by default,

1010
00:31:33,199 --> 00:31:35,721
leaving savings at zero interest,

1011
00:31:35,721 --> 00:31:38,396
paying late, and always buying new.

1012
00:31:38,745 --> 00:31:40,754
Angle two, the psychology.

1013
00:31:40,934 --> 00:31:43,342
Treating windfalls as fun money,

1014
00:31:43,342 --> 00:31:46,503
constant rewards, keeping up with friends,

1015
00:31:46,503 --> 00:31:48,158
living only for today,

1016
00:31:48,158 --> 00:31:49,889
avoiding your accounts,

1017
00:31:49,889 --> 00:31:51,695
believing it's too late,

1018
00:31:51,695 --> 00:31:54,254
believing more income will fix it,

1019
00:31:54,254 --> 00:31:57,339
and believing wealth is for other people.

1020
00:31:57,688 --> 00:31:59,407
Angle three, the playbook.

1021
00:31:59,587 --> 00:32:01,002
No emergency fund,

1022
00:32:01,002 --> 00:32:02,888
saving what's left over,

1023
00:32:02,888 --> 00:32:04,382
skipping the match,

1024
00:32:04,382 --> 00:32:07,054
never investing, no written goals,

1025
00:32:07,054 --> 00:32:10,120
never negotiating, skipping protection,

1026
00:32:10,120 --> 00:32:11,928
and doing it all alone.

1027
00:32:12,278 --> 00:32:13,985
Here's what I want you to remember.

1028
00:32:14,165 --> 00:32:16,405
Being broke is rarely about one big

1029
00:32:16,405 --> 00:32:16,917
failure.

1030
00:32:17,097 --> 00:32:19,563
It's usually a set of quiet habits,

1031
00:32:19,563 --> 00:32:22,171
and quiet habits can be replaced with

1032
00:32:22,171 --> 00:32:23,157
quiet systems.

1033
00:32:23,337 --> 00:32:25,141
You don't need a bigger income to

1034
00:32:25,141 --> 00:32:26,836
start, and you don't need to be

1035
00:32:26,836 --> 00:32:27,273
perfect.

1036
00:32:27,453 --> 00:32:29,001
You just need to be a little

1037
00:32:29,001 --> 00:32:30,272
better than last month,

1038
00:32:30,272 --> 00:32:30,936
every month.

1039
00:32:31,116 --> 00:32:32,753
You don't need to fix all twenty

1040
00:32:32,753 --> 00:32:33,008
five.

1041
00:32:33,188 --> 00:32:35,090
Pick one from each angle and start

1042
00:32:35,090 --> 00:32:35,650
this week.

1043
00:32:35,830 --> 00:32:37,486
If you want the full step by

1044
00:32:37,486 --> 00:32:39,319
step plan, it's in my playbook,

1045
00:32:39,319 --> 00:32:40,857
linked in the description.

1046
00:32:41,037 --> 00:32:42,663
And tell me in the comments which

1047
00:32:42,663 --> 00:32:44,044
habit you're breaking first.

1048
00:32:44,224 --> 00:32:45,443
I'm Ryan Rover.

1049
00:32:45,623 --> 00:32:46,970
See you in the next one.
